      "c-218d3ade4082206b": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-208",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "To go barefoot was a sign of distress and humiliation. Thus David went up Mount Olivet when he left Jerusalem at the time of Absalom’s rebellion (2 Sam. 15:30). The humiliation of the Egyptians was represented by the prediction of their walking barefoot (Isa. 20:2-4). When Ezekie…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-208",
          "sourceHash": "2f4a7fc161740ed67effdbcda98c00e3efa6bd0dbff17a46c276397cc91222cb",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-80ca4988624196cd": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-207",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Threshing was sometimes done by instruments, (see note on Isa. 28:27-28 #508) and sometimes by having the grain trampled underfoot by horses or oxen. This is still a common mode in the East. The cattle are driven over the grain, treading heavily as they go, and in this rude, wast…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-207",
          "sourceHash": "2e3b0299b905630c1a2c7d037d0a2063a1df7c0f931f90f7fce19e09fad144e0",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      }
    },
